---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import MiniSim from '../../components/calc/MiniSim';
import FiftyPicker from '../../components/calc/FiftyPicker';
import { FUNDS, fund, pct, bn, day, quarterLabel, META } from '../../lib/data';
import { fiftyTest, afterTax } from '../../lib/engine/tax';
import { src, related, fundHref } from '../../lib/common';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';
const govs = FUNDS.filter((f) => ['government', 'treasury', 'treasury-only'].includes(f.k));
const fails = govs.filter((f) => fiftyTest(f.q) === 'fails');
const close = govs.filter((f) => { const m = Math.min(...Object.values(f.q)); return m >= 0.45 && m < 0.55; });
const bigFails = [...fails].sort((a, b) => b.assets - a.assets).slice(0, 8);
const failAssets = fails.reduce((s, f) => s + f.assets, 0);
const sno = fund('SNOXX'), sns = fund('SNSXX');
// Same example as every calculator on the site (test naif tour 2: $150,000 and $100,000 here, $120,000 and $50,000 elsewhere).
const sit = { amount: 50000, state: 'CA', filing: 'single' as const, income: 120000 };
const cSno = afterTax(sno, sit), cSns = afterTax(sns, sit);
const [q1, q2] = META.quarters;
const pickable = [...govs].sort((a, b) => b.assets - a.assets).map((f) => ({ t: f.t, name: f.name, q: f.q, href: fundHref(f.t) }));
const title = fitTitle(['50% Rule for Money Market Funds in CA, CT and NY: Who Fails', '50% Rule for Money Market Funds in CA, CT and NY: Who Passes']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `The 50% rule for money market funds in California, Connecticut and New York: how the quarter-end test works and which funds failed it in their own filings.`,
  `The 50% rule for money market funds in California, Connecticut and New York: how the quarter-end test works and which funds failed it in their own SEC filings.`,
]);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What counts toward the 50% for California?', a: `Obligations of the United States and of California whose interest the state cannot or does not tax: Treasury bills, notes and bonds, and debt of agencies such as the Federal Home Loan Banks and Farm Credit Banks. Repurchase agreements, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac securities, and bank or company paper do not count.` },
  { q: 'If a fund fails the 50% test, is any of the dividend exempt?', a: `Not in California, Connecticut or New York: the exemption for the year is lost on the whole dividend, including the part that came from Treasuries. In states without the test, such as New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Illinois, the federal share stays exempt whatever the fund’s mix.` },
  { q: 'How do I know if my fund met the test?', a: `The fund company’s year-end tax information usually lists, fund by fund, the percentage of income from U.S. obligations and whether the fund met the 50% requirement of California, Connecticut and New York. This site estimates the answer from the quarter-end holdings; the company’s statement is the one to rely on.` },
  { q: 'Can a Treasury money fund fail the 50% test?', a: `Yes, if it holds Treasuries mostly through repurchase agreements. Schwab Treasury Obligations (SNOXX) held ${pct(sno.q[q1], 1)} and ${pct(sno.q[q2], 1)} of its assets in Treasuries directly at the ${quarterLabel(q1)} and ${quarterLabel(q2)} quarter ends; the rest was repo backed by Treasuries, which does not count.` },
];
---
<GuideLayout lang="en" id="fifty-percent-rule" title={title} description={description} breadcrumbLabel="50% rule"
  h1="The 50% rule: which money funds keep their state tax exemption"
  intro="Under the 50% rule for money market funds, California, Connecticut and New York leave untaxed the part of a dividend earned on U.S. government debt only if the fund held at least half its money in that debt at each quarter end. The filings show who passes."
  faqs={faqs} related={related('california', 'new-york', 'connecticut')} sources={src('ftb-ca', 'ny-it201i', 'ct-ps', 'nmfp', 'fhlb-statute')}>
  <p>Federal law forbids states to tax interest on obligations of the United States, and a mutual fund that earns such interest can pass the exemption to its shareholders. Most states let it through in proportion: if 60% of a fund’s income came from Treasuries, 60% of its dividend is exempt. California, Connecticut and New York add a condition. The fund must have held at least 50% of its total assets in qualifying obligations at the close of each quarter of its taxable year. A fund that meets the test passes through its exempt share as usual. A fund that misses it at even one quarter end passes through nothing, and the whole dividend for the year is taxed by those three states. The rule turns a gradual question into a cliff. In the {govs.length} retail government and Treasury share classes behind this site, {fails.length} held less than 50% in qualifying obligations at the {quarterLabel(q1)} or {quarterLabel(q2)} quarter end, {bn(failAssets)} of assets in all, and {close.length} were within five points of the line on one of the two dates.</p>

  <div data-simulateur-secondaire><FiftyPicker client:load funds={pickable} quarters={[[q1, quarterLabel(q1)], [q2, quarterLabel(q2)]]} def="SPAXX" /></div>
  <p><strong>What it costs, in one example.</strong> A single Californian with $120,000 of taxable income and $50,000 in Schwab Treasury Obligations (SNOXX), which filed a yield of {pct(sno.y)}, kept {pct(cSno.afterTaxYield)} after federal and California tax. The same money in Schwab U.S. Treasury (SNSXX), which filed {pct(sns.y)} and held only Treasuries, kept {pct(cSns.afterTaxYield)}. The two funds sit side by side on the same platform and paid almost the same before tax.</p>
  <details class="not-prose my-4 rounded-lg border border-navy-200 px-3 py-2"><summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-medium text-navy-700">Have the figures from a fund report? Type them yourself</summary>
  <div data-simulateur-secondaire><MiniSim client:load kind="fifty" href="/en/" /></div>
  </details>

  <h2>What counts toward the money fund 50% rule, and what does not</h2>
  <p>The test counts obligations whose interest the state may not tax. Treasury bills, notes and bonds count. So does debt of a short list of agencies that Congress exempted from state tax: the Federal Home Loan Banks, whose statute shields their notes from state income tax, the Farm Credit Banks and the Tennessee Valley Authority. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac securities do not count, though they are government-sponsored, because their charters do not carry the exemption. Repurchase agreements (repo) do not count either, and this is where most government funds lose ground. In a repo, the fund lends cash overnight to a bank or dealer and takes Treasuries or agency securities as collateral. The interest comes from the borrower, so it is not interest on a U.S. obligation, and the asset on the fund’s books is the loan, not the collateral. A government fund can therefore hold nothing but government paper in the sense of rule 2a-7 (the federal rule for money funds) and still fall far below 50% for state tax purposes.</p>
  <p>The holdings schedule in each Form N-MFP report is detailed enough to run the test: every security is listed with its issuer, its category and its value. This site classes each holding as Treasury, exempt agency, other agency, repo by type of collateral, municipal or other, and sums the first two as a share of total holdings at each quarter-end report.</p>

  <h2>Who failed in the data</h2>
  <p>The largest share classes below 50% at one of the two quarter ends were these (share of the fund’s money in U.S. government debt that the three states cannot tax). Several are among the biggest retail government funds in the country, and several carry the word Treasury or Government in their name.</p>
  <div data-chrome class="overflow-x-auto"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Fund</th><th class="text-right">{quarterLabel(q1).replace(/, \d{4}$/, '')}</th><th class="text-right">{quarterLabel(q2).replace(/, \d{4}$/, '')}</th><th class="text-right">Assets</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{bigFails.map((f) => { const h = fundHref(f.t); return <tr><td>{h ? <a href={h}>{f.name}</a> : <strong>{f.name}</strong>}<span class="block text-xs">{f.t}</span></td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{pct(f.q[q1], 0)}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{pct(f.q[q2], 0)}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{bn(f.assets)}</td></tr>; })}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Limits of the check on this site</h2>
  <p>The law looks at the close of each quarter of the fund’s own taxable year. Many funds use the calendar year; others end their fiscal year in another month, and their quarters close on other dates. This site has the monthly reports, so it reads the calendar quarter ends ({quarterLabel(q1)} and {quarterLabel(q2)} in the current data) as a proxy. A fund near the line can pass on its own dates and fail on the calendar ones, or the reverse. The share is also measured on holdings at market value as reported, while the law speaks of total assets, which include a little cash and receivables. For a fund at 70% or 30%, none of this matters. For a fund at 48% or 52%, the year-end letter from the fund company decides, and it usually states plainly whether the fund met the California, Connecticut and New York requirement.</p>
  <p>Finally, the test is applied to every quarter of the year, and the current data covers only some of them. A fund that passed in June and September can still miss in December. The fund pages on this site show each quarter as it is filed; the after-tax figures assume that the quarters in the data are representative of the year.</p>

  <h2>What to do with the result</h2>
  <p>If your fund passed, the share of its dividend from U.S. obligations is subtracted on your state return: Schedule CA in California, the U.S. government interest line of Form IT-201 in New York, and the corresponding modification in Connecticut. The share to use is the one in the year-end letter, not the holdings estimate. If your fund failed, the whole dividend stays in your state income. Whether to change funds is your decision; the <a href="/en/">comparator on the home page</a> ranks every share class for your state with the test applied, and each fund page shows the quarter-end figures.</p>
</GuideLayout>
